    'body'        => <<<'HTML'
<p class="lead">On-demand jobs let you pick up work when you want it: a delivery shift tonight, a weekend warehouse assignment, a two-week design project. That flexibility is reshaping how people build careers, but the pay, taxes and protections vary a lot depending on how the work is set up. This guide breaks down the main types of on-demand work, what it really pays and how to choose the version that fits your life.</p>

<div class="key-takeaways"><p class="kt-title">Key takeaways</p><ul>
<li>On-demand jobs range from app-based gig work to short-term temp assignments through staffing agencies.</li>
<li>Gig apps usually pay you as a 1099 contractor, so you cover expenses and 15.3% self-employment tax yourself.</li>
<li>Temp jobs through an agency like Best Jobs Pro offer similar flexibility with W-2 pay, tax withholding and workplace protections.</li>
<li>Compare options on net hourly pay after expenses and taxes, not on the headline rate.</li>
</ul></div>

<h2 id="what-are-on-demand-jobs">What are on-demand jobs?</h2>
<p>On-demand jobs are short-term or task-based work you accept as it becomes available, instead of a fixed schedule set by one employer. You might claim a shift in an app, accept a project from a client or take a temp assignment that lasts a few days or a few months.</p>
<p>The common thread is control. You decide when to work, how much to work and often which jobs to accept. The trade-off is that income can be less predictable than a full-time salary, and some forms of on-demand work come without benefits.</p>
<p>On-demand work overlaps with what economists call the <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gig_economy" target="_blank" rel="noopener">gig economy</a>, but it is broader. It also includes temp staffing, per-diem healthcare shifts, seasonal work and freelance projects.</p>

<h2 id="why-flexible-work-is-growing">Why flexible work is growing</h2>
<p>Flexible work is not a fad. Several forces keep pushing more people toward it:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Smartphones made matching instant.</strong> On-demand work apps connect workers and customers in seconds, which used to take a phone tree or a classified ad.</li>
<li><strong>People want control over their time.</strong> Parents, students, caregivers and retirees often cannot commit to a rigid 40-hour week. Our guide to <a href="/flexible-scheduling-for-parents-in-todays-workforce/">flexible scheduling for parents</a> covers this in more detail.</li>
<li><strong>Extra income is a safety net.</strong> A second income stream cushions you against layoffs and rising costs.</li>
<li><strong>Employers need agility.</strong> Businesses staff up for peak seasons, special projects and absences without adding permanent headcount.</li>
<li><strong>Low barriers to entry.</strong> Many on-demand jobs need little more than a phone, a background check and reliable transportation.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="types-of-on-demand-jobs">Common types of on-demand jobs</h2>
<h3>Driving and delivery</h3>
<p>Rideshare, restaurant delivery, grocery shopping and package delivery are the most visible forms of gig work. They are easy to start and very flexible, but your car is your biggest expense, and earnings swing with demand.</p>
<h3>Local tasks and home services</h3>
<p>Furniture assembly, moving help, cleaning, yard work, pet sitting and small repairs. Workers with tools and trade skills can often charge more per hour than drivers.</p>
<h3>Online freelance work</h3>
<p>Writing, graphic design, web development, bookkeeping, virtual assistance, video editing and tutoring. Freelancers usually find clients through marketplaces; see <a href="/how-freelance-marketplaces-work/">how freelance marketplaces work</a> for a deeper look at fees and payments.</p>
<h3>Temp and short-term assignments</h3>
<p>Warehouse, light industrial, event staffing, administrative, customer service and general labor roles filled through staffing agencies. These can last a day or several months, and some convert to permanent positions.</p>
<h3>Specialized and per-diem work</h3>
<p>Nurses, IT specialists, accountants and skilled trades often work on-demand contracts or per-diem shifts at premium rates.</p>

<h2 id="temp-jobs-vs-gig-jobs">Temp jobs vs. gig jobs: the difference that matters most</h2>
<p>Both temp jobs and gig jobs are flexible, but they are set up very differently. The biggest difference is whether you are an employee (W-2) or an independent contractor (1099). That one detail decides who pays which taxes and what protections you get.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Gig work (app or marketplace)</th><th>Temp work (staffing agency)</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Typical tax status</td><td>1099 independent contractor</td><td>W-2 employee of the agency</td></tr>
<tr><td>Taxes withheld from pay</td><td>No, you pay estimated taxes</td><td>Yes, withheld every paycheck</td></tr>
<tr><td>Social Security and Medicare</td><td>You pay the full 15.3%</td><td>You pay 7.65%; employer pays 7.65%</td></tr>
<tr><td>Overtime pay</td><td>Not required</td><td>Generally required for non-exempt workers over 40 hours a week</td></tr>
<tr><td>Workers' compensation</td><td>Usually not covered</td><td>Typically covered</td></tr>
<tr><td>Unemployment insurance</td><td>Usually not eligible</td><td>Wages generally count toward eligibility</td></tr>
<tr><td>Work expenses</td><td>You pay them (deductible)</td><td>Usually minimal</td></tr>
<tr><td>Schedule control</td><td>Very high, minute to minute</td><td>High, you choose which assignments to accept</td></tr>
<tr><td>Path to permanent job</td><td>Rare</td><td>Common with temp-to-hire roles</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>Staffing agencies like Best Jobs Pro offer flexible temp work with W-2 protections, so you can choose assignments around your life without handling quarterly taxes or going without workers' comp. Many people use both: temp assignments for a steady base and gig apps to fill open evenings. You can see what is open right now on our <a href="/job-postings/">job postings</a> page.</p>

<h2 id="how-much-on-demand-jobs-pay">How much on-demand jobs pay</h2>
<p>How much on-demand jobs pay depends on the type of work, your city, the time of day and your skill level. Headline rates are also misleading, because gig workers pay their own expenses and taxes. The ranges below are rough gross estimates to help you compare, not guarantees.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Type of work</th><th>Rough gross pay</th><th>Main costs to subtract</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Rideshare and delivery</td><td>About $15 to $25 per active hour</td><td>Fuel, maintenance, insurance, depreciation, waiting time</td></tr>
<tr><td>Local tasks and handyman</td><td>About $20 to $60 per hour</td><td>Tools, supplies, travel, platform fees</td></tr>
<tr><td>Online freelance services</td><td>About $20 to $100+ per hour</td><td>Marketplace fees, software, unbilled admin time</td></tr>
<tr><td>Temp warehouse and general labor (W-2)</td><td>Varies by market and shift</td><td>Commute; taxes are withheld</td></tr>
<tr><td>Specialized per-diem work</td><td>Often above comparable salaried rates</td><td>Licensing, continuing education</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>

<h3>A quick net-pay example</h3>
<p>Say a delivery app shows you earned $24 an hour. If you drove 18 miles in that hour and your real vehicle cost is around 40 cents a mile, that is about $7 in car costs, leaving $17. Self-employment tax takes roughly 14% of that net (15.3% on 92.35% of earnings), and income tax comes on top. Your true take-home might land closer to $13 or $14 an hour. Compare that with a W-2 temp shift where the posted rate is your pay before standard withholding and there are no vehicle costs.</p>
<div class="callout callout--tip"><p><strong>Tip:</strong> Track hours from the moment you log in, not just time on a delivery or task. Waiting time is real time, and it is the number most gig workers underestimate.</p></div>

